{"step_index":12201,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T17:53:58Z","thinking":"Each tmux window appears to correspond to a distinct process, including the AI agent itself in window 0, a bash terminal in window 1, a linux shell in window 2, and a YouTube process in window 3, indicating a multi-process environment. The AI agent, labeled \"agy:0\", likely serves as the principal process managing these separate operations. This architecture allows the agent to handle various tasks concurrently, reflecting a complex operational setup.\n\n\nThe agent manages a set of concurrent processes, each tied to a specific task and accessible via a unique TTY. Each process utilizes an \"agy\" identifier, and the master agent (\"agy:0\") manages the resource allocation. These processes include those for an ERP system, Telegram bot, digital history tracking, kids content, a Rust task, article publishing, a 3D game studio, a YouTube pipeline, and a research & question-answering system.\n\n\nThe existing monitoring groups all \"agy\" processes generically, obscuring their individual identities and resource usage, which needs to be resolved. To differentiate each agent, its terminal (TTY), current working directory (CWD), or command line can be used as unique identifiers, enabling specific labeling in reports and graphs. The goal is to make the \"top_procs\" view show *which* specific agent is running and its individual resource usage.\n\n\nIf a process utilizes 100% of a single vCPU, its CPU usage will be represented as 100%. A 4-vCPU system theoretically enables a maximum cumulative usage of 400% across all cores. The graph scaling request suggests displaying CPU usage relative to this 400% maximum, rather than the 100% default. Adjustments are required to the graphing logic to reflect this 400% scaling for clarity in resource monitoring.\n\n\nThe \"in graph only\" comment strongly suggests the user wants a 400% scale specifically for visualization purposes, not a system-wide change. Displaying individual app CPU usage (e.g., 260%) requires a 400% scale; otherwise, data will be clipped. A 400% graph scale would clearly represent individual and combined CPU usage across all 4 vCPUs. This addresses the problem of off-scale values when a process utilizes multiple cores.\n\n\nA 400% scale for overall CPU usage will be implemented, calculated as `(data.cpu_overall / 100) * 400`, or the sum of all vCPU cores. To allow for flexibility, a toggle will be added to switch between the 400% scale and a 100% normalized scale. The 400% scale will be the default, as per the user's request.\n\nThe graph's Y-axis will range from 0% to 400%, with ticks at 0%, 100% (1 CPU), 200% (2 CPU), 300% (3 CPU), and 400% (4 CPU Max). Report cards will display both the 400% value and its 100% equivalent (e.g., `মোট CPU: 391% / ৪০০% (৯৭.৮% সার্বিক)`). Now, investigating the graph's interactivity.\n\n\nThe current implementation uses only tooltip hovering, needing an `onClick` handler for point selection and locking. The objective is to make selected points persist even after the mouse/finger interaction ends. This requires locking the selection and preventing live updates from overwriting the report card.\n\nThe click interaction must detect the data point index and store it in a locking variable. The report card and the graph itself will then be updated with the selected historical data, and a visual cue will indicate the locked point. Live updates will be suppressed when a point is locked.\n\n\nInvestigating how `server.py` retrieves system stats, specifically the API call to `http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/stats`. The returned data, containing CPU, RAM, and application-specific resource usage, will inform enhancements to the monitoring display. The goal is to determine if native stats within `server.py` are feasible or if enriching the existing data source is preferred. The \"apps\" section offers the potential for granular CPU usage breakdowns per process.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c \"\nimport subprocess, os, re\n\ndef get_tmux_mapping():\n    mapping = {}\n    try:\n        out = subprocess.check_output(['tmux', 'list-windows', '-t', 'agy', '-F', '#{window_index}:#{window_name}:#{pane_tty}:#{pane_current_path}'], text=True)\n        for line in out.strip().split('\\n'):\n            if line:\n                parts = line.split(':')\n                if len(parts) >= 4:\n                    idx, name, tty, cwd = parts[0], parts[1], parts[2], parts[3]\n                    mapping[tty] = (name, cwd)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print('tmux error:', e)\n    return mapping\n\nprint('TMUX mapping:', get_tmux_mapping())\n\"","Cwd":"/home/azureuser/IrakIroan/azure_file_explorer","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Testing tmux mapping extraction","toolSummary":"Test tmux mapping script"}}]}
